The rule is mainly about what happens when certain negative/restrictive adverbs come at the beginning of a sentence.

The important words in this rule include:

never
hardly
scarcely
rarely
seldom
little
etc.

The key idea is:

When such a negative/restrictive adverb is placed at the beginning, inversion takes place.

That means the helping verb comes before the subject.



1. NEVER

Rani Ma'am gives these examples:

(i) Normal sentence

He never helps me. ✅

Structure:

Subject + Adverb + Verb

He + never + helps

This is normal word order.

(ii) NEVER at the beginning — WRONG

❌ Never he does help me.

Why wrong?

Because once Never is placed at the beginning, we need inversion.




(iii) Correct

✅ Never does he help me.

Look at the change:

Normal:

He does help me never.

Inverted:

Never does he help me.

So:

Never + Helping Verb + Subject + Main Verb

Important:

Never does he help me. ✅

Not:

Never he does help me. ❌




2. What exactly is inversion?

Normally:

He does help me.

Order:

Subject → Helping Verb

But after a negative/restrictive adverb at the beginning:

Never does he help me.

Order becomes:

Helping Verb → Subject

That's called inversion.

Normal

He does help me.

Inversion

Never does he help me.




3. Example with HAVE

Rani Ma'am gives:

Never have I met a person like him. ✅

Let's break it:

Never → negative adverb
have → helping verb
I → subject
met → main verb

So:

Never + have + I + met...

Not:

❌ Never I have met...

This is the same inversion rule.



4. HARDLY

Rani Ma'am also gives:

He hardly attended the classes.

This is the normal sentence.

Normal:

He hardly attended the classes. ✅

hardly means almost not / scarcely.

Incorrect inversion

❌ Hardly he did attend the classes.

Again, the subject he cannot come before the helping verb did after initial hardly.

Correct

✅ Hardly did he attend the classes.

Structure:

Hardly + did + subject + V1



